## Phase 4 — Tables and data tables

Goal: on every screen the user can read **all** columns and **all** cell content, without the whole page scrolling sideways.

### 4.1 Always: contain horizontal overflow
Wrap the table in a scroll container so only the table scrolls, never the page:

```html
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Orders table" tabindex="0">
  <table>…</table>
</div>
```
```css
.table-scroll {
  overflow-x: auto;
  overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
  max-width: 100%;
}
.table-scroll table {
  width: 100%;
  min-width: max-content; /* or a rem value sized to the columns */
  border-collapse: collapse;
}
```
- `role="region"` + `aria-label` + `tabindex="0"` makes the scroll area keyboard-scrollable and announced to screen readers.
- Make sure every ancestor flex/grid item has `min-width: 0` (see Phase 2), otherwise the wrapper won't constrain.
- Add a scroll-shadow / fade affordance on the overflowing edge so users know more columns exist.

### 4.2 Reclaim horizontal space around tables
On small screens, page/section/card padding steals width from the table.
- Reduce `padding-inline` of the containing section/card at small breakpoints (e.g. `1.5rem` → `0.5rem`), or remove it for the table only.
- Optionally let the table scroller bleed to the viewport edges: `margin-inline: calc(-1 * var(--container-padding));` with matching inner padding on the first/last cells.
- Reduce cell padding on small screens (`padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem` → `0.375rem 0.5rem`) and slightly reduce font size via `clamp()` — never below ~0.8125rem for body data.
- Collapse redundant wrappers (card inside card) on mobile.

### 4.3 Wide columns and long cell content
Pick per column, based on what the data is:
- **Short, scannable values** (status, dates, amounts, IDs): `white-space: nowrap`; right-align numbers with `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`.
- **Free text** (descriptions, notes, addresses): allow wrapping, cap width with `max-width: 20rem–30rem` or `ch` units; optionally `-webkit-line-clamp` with an expand/tooltip.
- **Unbreakable strings** (URLs, emails, hashes, file paths): `overflow-wrap: anywhere;` (or `word-break: break-word`), or truncate with `text-overflow: ellipsis` + full value in a `title`/tooltip/copy button.
- **Truncation needs**: `overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap;` plus a fixed/max width — it does nothing on an auto-width cell. Use `table-layout: fixed` with explicit column widths when you need predictable truncation.
- Never truncate content without a way to see the full value.

### 4.4 Many columns — choose a strategy
Decide by column count and how users use the data; state which one you chose.

1. **Horizontal scroll with sticky key column** (default for data-heavy tables): `position: sticky; inset-inline-start: 0;` on the first column, with a background and z-index; sticky `thead th { position: sticky; top: 0; }` when the table also scrolls vertically. Note: sticky won't work if an ancestor between the cell and the scroller has `overflow: hidden`.
2. **Column priority / hiding**: hide low-priority columns below a breakpoint and expose them via an expandable row detail or a column-visibility toggle. Never silently drop data with no way to reach it.
3. **Stacked / card layout** for small screens (≤ ~5–6 columns, record-oriented data): each row becomes a card, each cell shows its header via `data-label`:
   ```css
   @media (max-width: 40rem) {
     .table-stack thead { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); }
     .table-stack tr { display: block; margin-block-end: 0.75rem; }
     .table-stack td { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; }
     .table-stack td::before { content: attr(data-label); font-weight: 600; }
   }
   ```
   Changing `display` on table elements can drop table semantics in some browsers; add `role="table"`, `role="row"`, `role="cell"`, `role="columnheader"` explicitly when using this pattern.
4. **Library-native responsive mode**: AG Grid column `minWidth`/`flex` + auto-size, MUI DataGrid `columnVisibilityModel`, Ant Table `scroll={{ x: 'max-content' }}` and `responsive: ['md']`, PrimeNG `scrollable` / `responsiveLayout="stack"`, DataTables.net Responsive extension. Prefer these over hand-written CSS.

### 4.5 Table chrome
- Toolbars (search, filters, bulk actions, export): `flex-wrap: wrap`; collapse secondary actions into a menu on small screens.
- Pagination: wrap or switch to compact (prev / page x of y / next) on small screens.
- Row action buttons: keep reachable (sticky end column or overflow menu); tap targets ≥ 44×44px.
- Tables inside modals, drawers, tabs, accordions, cards: the scroll wrapper must live *inside* the constrained container; modals need `max-width: min(100% - 2rem, …)`.
- Nested tables: give each its own scroll container or flatten on mobile.
- Virtualized grids: set column widths in config, not CSS; verify horizontal virtualization still renders sticky columns.
- Empty, loading (skeleton) and error states must fit the narrow width too.
- Print: `@media print` — remove scroll containers, allow wrapping, repeat `thead`.

